<Crumbs items={[['Home','/'],['Guides','/guides/'],['Smithery Alternatives']]} />
<h1>Smithery Alternatives: Where to Move Your MCP Servers</h1>
<p class="lead">Smithery is ending its hosted MCP proxy and connection services on October 31, 2026. Here is what changes, who needs to act, how to move each server, and the registries and hosts you can use instead.</p>
<p class="small">Updated Oct 29, 2026. Facts about Smithery come from <a href="https://www.smithery.ai/future" rel="noopener nofollow">Smithery's own notice</a>. Prices and limits come from each provider's site on the same day and can change.</p>

<div class="notice"><strong>Key Date: Oct 31, 2026.</strong> Smithery URLs on <code>run.tools</code> and <code>server.smithery.ai</code> stop working, along with Smithery accounts, API keys, the API, Connect, Toolbox, the CLI and the SDKs. smithery.ai stays online as a public directory.</div>

<h2 id="whats-changing">What's Changing at Smithery and When</h2>
<p>In its notice, Smithery says that on October 31, 2026 it is shutting down its connection management services, its MCP proxy and its managed identity. Smithery says it is now part of Arcade.</p>
<table class="sm-kv">
<tr><th>Stays</th><td>smithery.ai as a public directory of MCP servers, now powered by Arcade.dev, without proxying. A curated set of verified servers whose pages show direct MCP URLs, for example <code>mcp.linear.app/mcp</code> instead of <code>linear.run.tools</code> or <code>server.smithery.ai/linear</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Stops on Oct 31</th><td>Server URLs on run.tools and server.smithery.ai. The Smithery API, Connect and Toolbox. Smithery accounts and API keys. The Smithery CLI and SDKs, whose current versions are being marked deprecated.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Paid plans</th><td>Smithery says it is refunding every charge since August 5 and cancelling subscriptions. Refunds usually arrive within 5 to 10 business days.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="who-is-affected">Who's Affected</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Anyone using a hosted or remote server through Smithery.</strong> If a client config points at a <code>run.tools</code> or <code>server.smithery.ai</code> URL, that server stops responding on October 31.</li>
<li><strong>Smithery CLI users.</strong> The CLI is being deprecated. Any config entry that the CLI wrote, or that runs servers through the CLI or a Smithery key, needs replacing.</li>
<li><strong>Apps built on Smithery Connect, Toolbox, the API or SDKs.</strong> If you manage MCP connections for your own users through Smithery, Smithery says you need a replacement before October 31.</li>
<li><strong>Registry users.</strong> You can still browse smithery.ai to find servers. Connecting through Smithery will not be possible after October 31, so use the direct URL from the server's page.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="how-to-migrate">How to Migrate</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Find every Smithery reference.</strong> Search your MCP client configs for Smithery URLs and CLI calls. On macOS or Linux:
<div class="install"><pre>{grep}</pre></div>
Also check project files, CI secrets and environment variables for Smithery API keys.</li>
<li><strong>Look up each server's official URL or package.</strong> Smithery says verified server pages on smithery.ai now show the direct URL. You can also check the vendor's docs, the <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/" rel="noopener">Official MCP Registry</a>, or search <a href="/directory/mcp-servers/">MCP servers on Swarmnest</a>.</li>
<li><strong>If the vendor runs a remote server, connect to it directly.</strong> Replace the Smithery URL with the vendor's URL, then sign in again when your client prompts you (most official remote servers use OAuth). In Claude Code: <code>claude mcp add --transport http linear https://mcp.linear.app/mcp</code>.</li>
<li><strong>If there is no official remote server, run it locally.</strong> Use the vendor's own package with <code>npx</code>, <code>uvx</code> or its Docker image, or pick it from the <a href="https://hub.docker.com/mcp" rel="noopener">Docker MCP Catalog</a> and run it with the Docker MCP Toolkit. Prefer packages published by the vendor over unknown wrappers.</li>
<li><strong>If you need it hosted, self-host or move to another host.</strong> Deploy your own server on <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/model-context-protocol/guides/remote-mcp-server/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Workers</a>, deploy an open-source server on <a href="https://glama.ai/mcp/hosting" rel="noopener">Glama hosting</a>, or use a managed platform such as Composio, Pipedream MCP, Arcade or Zapier MCP for ready-made app integrations.</li>
<li><strong>If your product used Smithery Connect for your users,</strong> move the per-user auth to a platform built for it, such as <a href="/directory/auth-identity/pipedream-connect/">Pipedream Connect</a>, Composio or Arcade, and test the sign-in flow end to end.</li>
<li><strong>Test, then clean up.</strong> List tools and make a test call with <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector" rel="noopener">MCP Inspector</a> or your client, then remove the old Smithery entries and keys.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="alternatives">Smithery Alternatives Compared</h2>
<p>Smithery did two jobs: it was a <strong>registry</strong> where you found servers, and a <strong>host and proxy</strong> that ran them and handled sign-in. Most alternatives do one of those. Links marked "Swarmnest listing" go to our directory page for that tool.</p>
